WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:15.089
A delivery business buys a van. It will use that van for years. Every week it also pays for fuel and the driver's wages, and those are used up straight away. These are two very different kinds of spending.

00:00:15.650 --> 00:00:26.731
Capital expenditure is money spent buying or improving non-current assets that the business will use for more than one year. It is shown in the statement of financial position.

00:00:27.300 --> 00:00:37.994
Revenue expenditure is money spent on the everyday running of the business. Its benefit is used up within the current year, so it is an expense in the income statement.

00:00:39.100 --> 00:00:51.572
To define either term, first say what the money is spent on. Then develop it: how long the benefit lasts, or which financial statement shows it.

00:00:52.150 --> 00:00:58.542
For a difference question, write both halves in one sentence, joined by whereas. Each half earns a mark.

00:00:59.100 --> 00:01:08.380
If an example is asked, give a specific item for each. A van or a machine for capital. Wages, fuel or repairs for revenue.

00:01:09.500 --> 00:01:19.772
Here is a real question from November 2025. Explain the term revenue expenditure. Two marks.

00:01:20.350 --> 00:01:28.384
First the point. Revenue expenditure is money spent on the everyday running costs of the business, such as wages, fuel and repairs.

00:01:28.950 --> 00:01:42.463
Then develop it. Its benefit is used up within the current year, so it is recorded as an expense in that year's income statement. This explanation is by olevels dot com, not an official mark scheme.

00:01:43.600 --> 00:01:54.283
A common mistake is defining capital expenditure as money the owner puts into the business. That is capital, which is something different.

00:01:54.850 --> 00:02:02.800
Another is saying revenue expenditure is money earned. Expenditure always means money spent.

00:02:03.950 --> 00:02:14.231
Now you try. Kemi owns a bakery. Answer both parts about her spending. Pause here and work it out.

00:02:15.350 --> 00:02:29.103
Part a. Capital expenditure is money spent buying or improving non-current assets. The benefit lasts more than one year, so it is shown in the statement of financial position.

00:02:29.700 --> 00:02:37.536
Part b. A new oven is capital expenditure. The bakers' wages, or repairs to the oven, are revenue expenditure.

00:02:38.100 --> 00:02:46.934
Remember: say what the money is spent on, then how long the benefit lasts, or where it is shown. Now try the practice questions.
